WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:11.510
Malcolm Grant: May we then turn to item five,
which is the update on RightCare and the shift

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:16.150
to value-based healthcare? I would like to
invite Tim and Muir to come and join us. Can

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:20.640
I just say thank you on behalf of the whole
Board for taking the time to come and talk

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.680
to us, this morning? Paul will in a moment
introduce the theme.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.280
Paul Baumann: The Board is, of course, already
familiar with the RightCare programme from

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:53.249
previous discussions, which have highlighted
its central importance both in creating major

00:00:53.249 --> 00:00:57.670
improvements in outcomes, which lie very much
at the heart of what RightCare is all about,

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:02.739
but also at the same time delivering a very
substantial proportion of the efficiency gains

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:06.140
that we are committed to make and indeed need
to make in order to live within the resources

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.650
we have available in the Spending Review.
It is a programme which is absolutely central

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:16.470
to our success as an organisation and the
NHS’s success beyond that, and therefore,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:21.310
it is right that we take this opportunity
to have a proper review of it. It works hand

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:26.140
in glove with similar programmes led by NHS
Improvement, which focus on eliminating waste

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.460
and tackling variation in the delivery of
services by providers, notably, of course,

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.670
through the Getting It Right First Time initiative,
which should, by the way, Tim, win the award

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:40.430
for the most sonorous acronym in the public
sector – GIRFT – which I suspect probably

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:44.530
sounds better in my native West Country than
it does in London.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:48.890
We are making good progress with the roll-out
of both programmes across the country; following

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:53.030
our decision recently to ramp up RightCare
from a cottage industry to a fully-fledged

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:58.050
national programme with commensurate resources,
it is now engaged actively with every health

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:02.800
economy in the country. RightCare is also
being adopted by our specialised commissioning

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.190
teams at national and local level as a key
pillar of their efficiency plans over the

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:12.940
next few years, and we are so to speak injecting
RightCare into national efficiency programmes

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:18.300
in areas such as continuing healthcare and
medicines optimisation, so its reach is rapidly

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.460
growing.

00:02:19.469 --> 00:02:22.959
I am really pleased to welcome into the audience
today a number of members of the RightCare

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:28.310
team who are with us today, particularly as
it gives us an opportunity to express on behalf

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:33.909
of the Board and others our appreciation of
the enormous and very valuable work that they

00:02:33.909 --> 00:02:37.650
are doing, and I would very much like them
to pass that on to the quite numerous colleagues

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:43.209
who are not with us today. You are doing a
splendid job in an area of enormous importance.

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:48.829
As the paper points out, both RightCare and
GIRFT will be the key to the success of STPs

00:02:48.829 --> 00:02:55.590
going forward, not least because in most places,
STPs are best placed to oversee the resulting

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:00.439
reallocation of capacity, which is one of
the biggest challenges facing the programmes,

00:03:00.439 --> 00:03:04.549
but also a major opportunity, particularly
in the context of the operational pressures

00:03:04.549 --> 00:03:09.270
we will be discussing a little later. We are
at a crucial point in both programme, where

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:13.739
great opportunities need to be translated
into effective implementation on the ground

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:19.510
if the potential benefits, both clinical and
financial, are to be realised in practice.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:22.900
I put it to you that our question today is
not so much whether the programmes are good

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:28.900
concepts, or even whether they give us the
right scale of opportunity; on both of those

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:34.329
questions, there is wide consensus. The critical
question is how we capture those opportunities

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:40.200
at the pace required by the challenges faced
by the NHS over the next few years.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.200
Both RightCare and GIRFT rely critically on
a deep and evidence-based partnership between

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.040
clinical leaders and managers from a range
of functions, including, of course, finance,

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.019
which is why Bruce and I have been heavily
involved in sponsoring the RightCare programme

00:03:54.019 --> 00:03:58.389
within NHS England. It is also why we have
invited two of the clinicians who have been

00:03:58.389 --> 00:04:02.870
central to the development of these programmes
to come and inform our discussion by sharing

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:08.099
with us their perspectives on the best way
to capture the enormous opportunity on offer.

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:12.409
Muir Gray is an Oxford professor, but we will
not hold that against him; a former Chief

00:04:12.409 --> 00:04:17.959
Knowledge Officer of the NHS; and an internationally
acknowledged expert on value-based healthcare,

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:23.310
with a 45-year track record of leadership,
innovation and prolific authorship in this

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:27.600
area. More specifically, he is, of course,
one of the founding fathers of what has now

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.330
become NHS RightCare, so we have him to thank
for the programme that we are now taking forward.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:38.260
Tim Briggs is a leading orthopaedic surgeon
based at the RNOH, with a similar track record

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:43.610
of national leadership in this field. In 2015,
he was appointed National Director for Clinical

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:49.190
Quality and Efficiency, and he was a key contributor
to the efficiency review led by Lord Carter.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:53.640
The RNOH itself was the cradle of the GIRFT
programme, with Tim I would say as a somewhat

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:59.980
unlikely midwife, so we are again indebted
to him for the ground-breaking work we are

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:03.010
now benefiting from. Without more ado, welcome,
Muir and Tim. The floor is yours.

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:07.980
Muir Gray: The mission of RightCare is to
get better outcomes for individuals and populations

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:14.510
with no more resources. I use the word ‘resource,’
because it is not just money; it could be

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:20.190
the time of staff, it could be the time of
patients, it could be imaging department capacity,

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:24.540
it could be the lab department, it could be
the number of beds, as Tim will discuss. There

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:29.901
has been in the last month a huge surge and
increase in value-based healthcare: seven

00:05:29.901 --> 00:05:40.190
papers in the Lancet, two BMG editorials,
and a report by the OECD chaired by Jeremy

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:47.130
Hunt saying that three dollars out of ten
are not producing value for the population

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:51.340
served; not specifically, obviously, the UK,
but we are in the lead in the thinking and

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.200
the practice.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.460
We use the word ‘value’ as distinct from
‘efficiency.’ Efficiency is outcomes over

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:04.090
costs for the patient treated, but value also
includes allocative value – do we have the

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:09.420
right distribution of resources to cancer
or mental health, or within cancer, to breast

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:15.370
or cervix or lung – and secondly, the use
of resources has to take into account the

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:20.330
underuse of high-value interventions like
the treatment of atrial fibrillation with

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:26.470
warfarin, and the overuse of lower-value,
zero-value or negative interventions, so it

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:29.240
is about a shift in resources.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:35.310
We have 20 delivery partners working with
the CCGs, and increasingly with the providers

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:41.000
and the clinicians. A couple of examples:
in Bradford, they have improved not only the

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:47.550
practice but the outcome of cardiovascular
disease, and released £1.6 million for reallocation.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:53.310
In Croydon, working closely with Tim’s team,
the same for musculoskeletal: again, £1 million

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:58.840
released fairly simply for reallocation. We
do not like the word ‘savings,’ actually,

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:02.520
unless someone has actually overspent; we
find the clinicians are turned off by the

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:07.890
word ‘savings,’ so we are looking all
the time for the words ‘value’ and ‘reallocation.’

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:13.910
There is terrific scope still; a couple of
examples. Imaging has gone up 12% a year in

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:20.700
many places; in one London hospital, a 22%
increase in CT and MRI. It is a huge increase;

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:25.860
what is the added-value of the last 10% of
that? We also have a project going led by

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:29.890
a terrific young doctor called Ben Goldacre,
whom you may have seen on television or read

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:35.690
in the Guardian. He has developed a browser-based
means of looking at how every practice operates;

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:44.920
they just pick one drug, and if every GP took
the equally effective lower-cost drug, it

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:50.941
would free up resources for 350 district nurses
and 350 care assistants. A district nurse

00:07:50.941 --> 00:07:57.970
plus a care assistant; 76,000 is our basic
unit of high-value care, so huge changes from

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:00.140
multiple small changes.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:07.670
Looking into the future, how do we see it?
I think we see it still as largely a culture

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:12.639
change. Obviously, we are working in management
with the teams here, or part of the teams

00:08:12.639 --> 00:08:17.170
here, but it is changing the way the clinicians
and the public think about the balance of

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:25.540
benefit to harm. Key to this is the way individual
patients are informed about the risks as well

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:30.470
as the benefits of harm. Modern medicine is
fantastic, what I have seen happen in the

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:34.820
50 years since I went to medical school, but
all healthcare does harm, and do the patients

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:39.950
really, really understand the risks as well
as the benefits? The answer is, not yet, but

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:44.120
we have a shared decision-making service which
we should increasingly automate, and no-one

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:48.800
should have a significant intervention without
being fully informed about the risks as well

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:50.360
as the benefits.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:57.450
Secondly, we will continue work on variation,
and we will not do this much more light-touch.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:03.430
This is the NHS Atlas, which was produced
on the principle of OMG: Oh My God, what is

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:08.450
going on here? We do not know what right is
for many of these issues, and the third Atlas

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:13.910
is upstairs for you, I think. We want to move
much faster now. Say it is skin disease; it

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:20.640
is a small budget, £2 billion a year. Fee
maps would allow the whole of the Health Service

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.260
to think, so we are moving much more to think
of different ways of getting variation.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.910
Secondly, programme budgeting, and the Programme
Budgeting Board will be chaired by Matthew

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:40.430
Cripps. There is a 1.9-fold variation in spend
on mental health; that is a lot of money,

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:48.050
and not only can I find no good reason, but
I still feel the CCGs plus specialist commissioning

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:52.529
are not really addressing the issues of what
we call between-programme allocation, and

00:09:52.529 --> 00:09:57.150
that is going to be a priority for Paul’s
team and the other teams in the next year.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:10:02.380
Thirdly, systems, so once you get into the
programme, you need to forget about primary,

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:07.740
secondary and tertiary; they are vitally important,
but they are bureaucracies. Very important,

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:13.250
but we need population-based programmes and
population-based systems. Is care for people

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:18.220
with atrial fibrillation better in Liverpool
or Manchester? Do not know. Is care for people

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:21.920
with asthma better in Somerset or Devon? We
know a bit about outcomes, but not related

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:28.320
to the cost, so we have to shift to a vertical
programming matrix, and that is part of the

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:32.560
work we are doing, and atrial fibrillation
is a good case study in that. We could probably

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:39.430
prevent 5,000 strokes, and 10% faster dementia
with no more resources, if they managed atrial

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:45.420
fibrillation as well as they do in Bradford.
These are the sorts of challenges we are facing.

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:49.380
Then the issue of culture – and this is
where Tim and I worked together – as many

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:54.560
of you around the Board will know from your
management experience, culture eats structural

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:59.860
reorganisation for breakfast, lunch, dinner
and afternoon tea. We see this largely as

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:04.740
a cultural programme, and Tim’s work is
also culture change, too. We are thinking

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:11.029
of, how do we really influence how large numbers
of people think? That requires a common language.

00:11:11.029 --> 00:11:18.430
Does everyone use the words ‘value’ and
‘efficiency’ in the same way? The answer?

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:22.120
No. Who do we have to train? Probably down
to about 8D people. If we were in the military

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:26.480
or we were in big business, then we would
move into a much more systematic approach

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:30.480
to culture change, and that is one of the
things that the Board might like to ask about.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:34.600
In all of this, we have been working increasingly
closely with the terrific work Tim and his

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:37.670
team are doing, so I will pass to Tim now.

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:43.971
Tim Briggs: I know that some of the Board
are really unsighted, on me, really, and on

00:11:43.971 --> 00:11:47.580
what we do, so I thought I would just over
the next two minutes tell you where we started

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:52.010
from and exactly where we are going. I am
an orthopaedic surgeon based at the Royal

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:56.380
National Orthopaedic Hospital. I have done
26 years at the coalface in a tertiary referral

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:59.960
hospital, and I could see what was coming
down the track, because I was also Medical

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:04.371
Director at the time. We have an ageing population
who are living on; we all know that. People

00:12:04.371 --> 00:12:09.899
want to remain independent, and therefore,
the burden of MSK disease is getting greater

00:12:09.899 --> 00:12:17.200
every year, and we were seeing referrals climbing
at 7% to 8% per annum. If you look at orthopaedics,

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:23.570
we represent 33% of the surgical workforce,
25% rise – 26% now – in all surgical interventions

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:29.041
in the secondary care centre are in my specialty,
so we are massive players in the game. What

00:12:29.041 --> 00:12:33.250
I was seeing was patients coming through to
us, who maybe had not had the best advice,

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:38.580
had not had really the best outcome, and so
I wrote a report that was published in 2012

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:44.490
focused on improving quality, improving patient
safety, and with that, you would squeeze out

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:49.149
unwarranted variation, reduce complications,
and actually, from that, you get better outcomes

00:12:49.149 --> 00:12:53.621
and actually savings would fall out of the
bottom. That is what I thought.

00:12:53.621 --> 00:13:00.470
I produced this report, and Bruce was very
kind in getting me £200,000 plus VAT; he

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:06.190
never lets me forget about the VAT. With that,
we got a team of five together, and we got

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:11.360
a unique dataset on every single trust in
England, or 12 different sets of data based

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:16.420
on quality outcomes, actually. Then the great
strength of what we are doing, it is clinically

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:20.760
led and it was a peer-to-peer review, and
then I went out with my team, having sent

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:25.480
the data to every hospital, went to visit
every hospital. In the last two years, I have

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:30.570
been to every single hospital in England,
Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, so no-one

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:35.540
can pull the wool over me; I know exactly
what is going on out there. The idea was to

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:40.180
have this conversation with clinicians and
senior management teams about what they were

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:44.200
doing, how well they were doing, and if they
were not doing things well, what we could

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:46.350
do to improve it.

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:51.350
What we found out there is that there was
significant variation; there was some excellence

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:56.480
in some places, and there was no doubt massive
scope to tackle this variation. I am just

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:01.980
going to give you a number of examples which
I think will demonstrate what we can do, and

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:06.640
how it can help you. If you look at the deep
infection rate for hip and knee replacement

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:12.480
across the country, just in England, it varies
from 0.2% to 5%. There was one hospital in

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:19.350
London that we went to where, at the time,
they had a bit of a problem, and it was 15%.

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:24.910
It costs on average to treat an infected hip
or knee replacement anywhere between £75,000

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:32.380
and £100,000, by the time you take into account
all costs. If we could reduce that infection

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:38.160
rate, which we can do by setting up certain
standards – ringfenced beds, etc. – to

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:46.600
less than 0.5%, you would save the NHS every
year between £250 million and £300 million.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:52.620
That means you could do between 40,000 and
60,000 extra joint replacements at no extra

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:58.991
cost. We also found, arthroscopy rates in
patients over 60 who then go and have a knee

00:14:58.991 --> 00:15:05.200
replacement within a year, the variation was
very significant. We also found surgeons doing

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:08.950
low volumes of procedures, and if you are
doing low volumes, your outcomes are not as

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:11.959
good, which means the patient will be back
earlier for revision, and if you are doing

00:15:11.959 --> 00:15:17.240
volumes, you are getting what we call loan
kit costs to do these procedures, and we found

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:23.380
loan kit costs around the country per trust
was varying anywhere between £250,000 a year

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:30.190
to £760,000 a year. Absolutely crazy. When
you drill down to what surgeons are doing,

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:35.420
81% of surgeons doing revision knee replacement,
which is complex and expensive, were doing

00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:40.899
less than ten a year. In half knee replacements,
we know you have got go up a learning curve

00:15:40.899 --> 00:15:45.410
to do about 20 a year, to get up that learning
curve. The commonest number being done was

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:51.370
one, followed by two, and 75% of surgeons
were doing fewer than ten a year.

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:55.170
We also looked at litigation costs. In orthopaedics,
it is going through the roof; we are the most

00:15:55.170 --> 00:16:00.820
litigated out of all the surgical specialties.
We looked at the dataset for 2011 and 2012.

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:09.180
If you go back to 2003/2004, we paid out in
one year £39 million for litigation orthopaedics.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:14.410
In 2011/2012, it was £187 million, and it
is climbing at the rate of £30 million to

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:19.530
£40 million a year. What we did is, we took
the data from 2011/2012, we drilled down to,

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:25.360
well, what is the cost in England of a patient’s
cost of every intervention, what litigation

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:30.510
cost was it? It was £54. We went to trusts
around the country where it varied in that

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:37.959
year from zero, and also trusts where, for
every single case, it was £154. At no point

00:16:37.959 --> 00:16:42.200
had there been any discussion between management
and clinicians to address these issues, and

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:49.630
what we found was that if you were a high
outlier, it was a recurring problem. We found

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:54.740
that we could do a huge amount in terms of
what we could do, and what is interesting

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:58.050
is, that since we have been round, if you
look at the litigation data now, which I have

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:07.460
– and we started our visits in 2013 – in
2013/2014, orthopaedic claims climbed by 9%,

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:10.820
which we would rather expect, because we were
going round the country at that time, but

00:17:10.820 --> 00:17:17.799
at 2014/2015, they have dropped by 5%, and
at 2015/2016, they have dropped by 9%. A real

00:17:17.799 --> 00:17:23.119
evidence base that what we are doing has an
impact, and it is because it is peer-to-peer

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:24.300
clinical review.

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:33.550
After that £250,000, we were then able from
the Department of Health to get £2.55 million,

00:17:33.550 --> 00:17:38.520
which has allowed us to implement the findings
in orthopaedics. What that means is, we have

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:42.710
new datasets, we are going back out round
to every single trust in England, we have

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:46.960
comparison reports, see what they are doing,
how they have moved the dial. There is some

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:51.970
really good evidence there; let me give you
two examples. One is the Homerton. It was

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:56.130
a bit of a problem two or three years ago,
and they have a completely new set of young

00:17:56.130 --> 00:18:00.710
consultants there. They are moving the dial
very, very quickly, in terms of their choice

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:05.811
of implants, sub-specialising, doing what
is right, using the evidence base, etc. Another

00:18:05.811 --> 00:18:10.110
example is Ashford and St Peter’s, where
they have moved the dial significantly since

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:16.711
we were there. We will also be producing dashboards,
which will have all that dataset, which we

00:18:16.711 --> 00:18:20.880
will get trusts to refresh, and that will
form part of the model hospital dashboard

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:24.550
and the portal, so every trust will be able
to see what it is doing, and then we want

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:29.480
to allow trusts to compare their outcomes
with other trusts.

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:34.049
We also are able to look at another 11 surgical
specialties, so this is just what is coming

00:18:34.049 --> 00:18:39.410
your way at the moment. Of those, we have
appointed 11 national clinical leads, who

00:18:39.410 --> 00:18:44.820
are like myself, I hope, and who are going
to deliver, using the same methodology I have

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:50.100
done, the datasets, going out there, peer-to-peer
review, and the solutions actually become

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:54.771
obvious, and therefore there will be local
reports and also national reports. Just to

00:18:54.771 --> 00:18:58.460
give you an idea of the calibre of people
who have now come forward as these national

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:03.590
clinical leads, the current President of the
Royal College of Ophthalmologists applied

00:19:03.590 --> 00:19:08.790
for and got the GIRFT lead job. That is the
sort of calibre of people we have, and my

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:16.730
team are brilliant. The team is now going
up from a team of five to 122, because we

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:22.120
were able to get another £60 million from
the government on 8th November last year,

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:27.800
and so we are now going to look at 30 specialties,
which covers 92% of all the work that goes

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:29.920
on in the provider sector.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:33.420
Where are we at the moment? We have general
surgery out on the road; John Abercrombie

00:19:33.429 --> 00:19:37.860
is my National Clinical Lead. The national
report, I have seen the first draft; it is

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:41.799
going to be a wake-up call, I have to say,
for clinicians. It is going to be a wake-up

00:19:41.799 --> 00:19:47.830
call for the NHS. Behind that, we have spinal
surgery. That is going to be a massive wake-up

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:52.680
call for all of us, in terms of using the
evidence base on what we do, and a lot of

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:57.130
what goes on in the spinal surgery – about
injections and some of this instrumented fusion

00:19:57.130 --> 00:20:03.070
– the evidence is not great, and there was
a national spinal registry mandated six years

00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:11.470
ago by CCGs; to date, only 15% of the total
data has been inputted. 80% of spinal surgeons

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:16.679
do not know their infection rate. We also
have vascular surgery out there, we have neurosurgery

00:20:16.679 --> 00:20:21.900
– those visits have taken place – and
again, the national report is going to be

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:26.230
a wake-up call for all of us in the provider
side, and also for the NHS.

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:32.020
Let me give you some examples. If you have
your gall bladder removed and you have it

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:37.690
done in any trust in England, your readmission
rate back into hospital at 30 days varies

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:43.290
from 0% to 12%. If you are unfortunate enough
to get a cancer of your lower bowel and you

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:51.120
need a stoma, your stoma rate after 18 months
varies between 25% to 78%. If you have your

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:55.860
spinal surgery and you ask your surgeon, ‘What
is my infection rate,’ they do not know.

00:20:55.860 --> 00:21:00.980
If you have an oral cancer of the mouth excised,
your re-admission for a further operation

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:08.429
at 90 days varies from 8% to 80.56%. If you
have your tonsils taken out – you would

00:21:08.429 --> 00:21:14.370
have thought we would get tonsils right – your
readmission rate at 30 days varies from 3.6%

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:21.080
to 27%. Huge variation, and something that
I think we can really tackle and do something

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:25.890
about. If you look at neurosurgical procedures
and you have what is called a ventriculoperitoneal

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:31.870
shunt, your revision rate at two years varies
from 8% to 44%, and your infection rate from

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:34.990
0.2% to 6%.

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:40.700
Where are we now? We have all this data. The
national reports will be produced, and they

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:46.070
will actually change practice. Then I am now
sitting with NHSI, have the mandate from Jim

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:51.169
Mackey and also Patrick Carter, to deliver
this programme, which means using the data

00:21:51.169 --> 00:21:56.010
and not acting on the data now in trusts is
no longer an option, and we are going to hold

00:21:56.010 --> 00:22:01.410
people to account to deliver that. I think
it is a real opportunity for us all to make

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:06.040
the changes that we need to, to make sure
we improve quality, reduce unwanted variation

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:11.429
and complications, and with that, the savings
will drop out and allow us to do more work

00:22:11.429 --> 00:22:14.080
through our bed base that we currently have.

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:22.750
I also came across pockets of really good
care, where it is not really widely disseminated

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:27.950
across the NHS, and if I may, I will just
give you two examples of that. In Fife in

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:33.880
Scotland, they have a real problem with their
admissions of surgical patients, so they put

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:40.281
a general surgical consultant on call as the
first person to see a patient after A&amp;E. Guess

00:22:40.281 --> 00:22:45.090
what happens to their admission rate? It has
dropped by 30%. If you go to the Luton &amp; Dunstable

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:48.110
Hospital, which is near where I live, and
that is where I will go if I am poorly, I

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:54.740
assume, they had a problem with their medical
admissions. They hit their A&amp;E targets. Why?

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:58.891
Because they have addressed the problem. They
have put senior consultants behind the A&amp;E

00:22:58.891 --> 00:23:03.710
department who see these patients, and their
admission rates have dropped by 30%. There

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:08.750
are massive ways that I think we can actually
streamline our system and use our bed base

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:14.690
of 129,000 beds very much more effectively,
and help the NHS to continue to provide time-effective

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:16.530
care for our patients.

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:23.660
Malcolm Grant: I think two really well focused
and hugely informative presentations.

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:35.480
Noel Gordon: As you know, I am a great enthusiast
for all you are doing in improving our cost,

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:43.160
our quality and our outcomes around our triple
aim. Having spent much of my time in another

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:48.600
industry trying to do something very similar,
you quickly realise the distinction between

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:56.260
shining a light and embedding it in a culture
where it becomes a way of life and good working

00:23:56.260 --> 00:24:02.840
practice. What are you finding at the moment
about the way we can build this into continuous

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:08.650
improvement in the way we run our health system?
Are we getting closer to embedding it into

00:24:08.650 --> 00:24:12.480
the culture?
Muir Gray: Let me answer first quickly, a

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:20.360
little bit more to the clinician end of the
spectrum, we are one team. As I say, my own

00:24:20.361 --> 00:24:27.901
view is, it is culture, and I think there
are about 500 people per million of the population,

00:24:27.901 --> 00:24:32.500
most of them clinicians, who need a different
mental concept. For the last 20 years, it

00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:38.180
has been evidence-based decision-making, quality
improvement: both absolutely essential, but

00:24:38.180 --> 00:24:42.600
they now have to take responsibility for,
in the words of the Academy of Medical Royal

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:49.679
Colleges, the stewardship of resources. If
we want an NHS for the next generation, the

00:24:49.679 --> 00:24:55.110
clinicians have to take responsibility for
getting good-value use of resources, and this

00:24:55.110 --> 00:25:01.110
requires not circulars or documents: it requires
us to train people to think in a different

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:06.920
way, and that is our plan for the future.
We have not made the move to large-scale;

00:25:06.929 --> 00:25:13.930
people are still looking to, ‘Will the commissioner
sort it out? Will the medical director sort

00:25:13.930 --> 00:25:21.110
it out?’ Everyone who orders an MRI or a
second rheumatoid factor test or whatever,

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:25.830
has to feel, ‘I am committing resources
that might’ – in the words of the Academy

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:29.790
of Royal Colleges – ‘delay or deny treatment
to other patients.’ It is not the taxpayer

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:36.380
we are worried about; it is other patients.
I think we see the need for a major cultural

00:25:36.380 --> 00:25:38.130
thought-leadership, you would probably call
it.

00:25:38.130 --> 00:25:43.640
Tim Briggs: What I would say to that is, I
think we will drive the dial, and I think

00:25:43.640 --> 00:25:49.570
it is going to happen. The reason I say that
is twofold, really. One is, we now have all

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:54.900
the Royal Colleges, all the special societies,
everybody behind this, and they see it as

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:59.550
the serious game in town, because it is peer-to-peer
review, clinically led, and it will deliver

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:06.350
the change we need to make within the provider
side. Crucially, the data is robust. The first

00:26:06.350 --> 00:26:09.929
thing I was told always was, ‘Tim, the data
is not great,’ and actually, by the time

00:26:09.929 --> 00:26:14.810
you have 12 different sets of data, it tells
a really good story and the light bulb goes

00:26:14.810 --> 00:26:19.730
on, and they say, ‘Well, crikey, we ought
to do something about this.’ The dashboard

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:25.289
that we have developed in orthopaedics will
have one for each specialty on the model hospital

00:26:25.289 --> 00:26:30.660
website, and the portal actually will drive
the change. Especially when you let providers

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:35.350
look at other providers; no-one likes being
in the bottom 25%, and that will make a big

00:26:35.350 --> 00:26:40.430
difference. I think the fact that we are going
to have robust data: that will drive the change,

00:26:40.430 --> 00:26:45.570
and the fact these are peer-to-peer review.
Just going out now, when I am going back out

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:51.500
with all my follow-up data and my comparison
reports, everybody is at these meetings, the

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:55.890
senior management team are all there, everybody
is buying into it and they know that there

00:26:55.890 --> 00:27:02.289
are another 29 coming behind me. They now
know, through NHSI and with the work you are

00:27:02.289 --> 00:27:08.230
doing in NHSE, doing nothing with the data
is not an option anymore.

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:15.450
I think one of the things we need is much
closer working between management and clinicians.

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:19.860
Wherever I went, where it was top-down management,
it was not working; it is not going to work.

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:24.600
Everybody just gets disenfranchised and just
gives up. Actually, where you have clinicians

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:28.559
and managers working together shoulder to
shoulder, as I call it, then that is the way

00:27:28.559 --> 00:27:33.850
to live it, so I think we are seeing significant
change. I have lots of evidence from hospitals

00:27:33.850 --> 00:27:37.850
where they have moved the dial. Yes, I think
they are up for it, up for the challenge.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:46.620
Ian Dodge: I think it is just great. It is
about two years since we met, and I think

00:27:46.630 --> 00:27:51.070
it is fabulous. Matthew is in the audience,
and we should pay credit to the work that

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:59.160
he has done to build RightCare. What I am
intrigued by – and Tim, particularly – is,

00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:06.630
we are looking down the vertical specialties,
but there must be similarities across horizontally.

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:14.450
The question, I guess, is, why do we get this
variation, and what systemically do you think

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:25.000
needs to change in order to give us better
outcomes and better ability to drive out better

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:27.620
outcomes?
Tim Briggs: I think it is multi-factorial,

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:35.750
I would say that. I think if you go back to
the early 2000s, the driver was 18 weeks,

00:28:35.750 --> 00:28:40.120
and that was the sole driver of where we were
spending our money, and actually, that was

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:47.530
the target. Therefore, quality of outcome
was not an issue that was looked at, and I

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:52.561
think now, the focus with the way we are and
the situation now, there has to be a real

00:28:52.561 --> 00:28:58.090
focus on quality. I think as a profession,
we have been slowing the uptake, but I think

00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:02.180
we now have the bit between our teeth, and
I think what is shown from that is the support

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:09.690
we have had from all the colleges and associations.
I think we will now see with the good evidence

00:29:09.690 --> 00:29:13.230
base that we have for each trust and with
each specialty as we drive it, that actually,

00:29:13.230 --> 00:29:18.540
we will see the squeeze-out of unwanted variation.
I think it always calls into question the

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:25.110
way we train, and I think we have to look
right back at that. If you are a surgeon – because

00:29:25.110 --> 00:29:32.809
I think you ask a very good question – why
is there a such a difference? The mandate

00:29:32.809 --> 00:29:37.669
now for HEE includes working with us, with
our datasets, to make sure that trainees are

00:29:37.669 --> 00:29:45.200
placed where the outcomes are great, so great
service is usually great training, in my experience,

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:50.200
and I am still at training programme data
with 60 trainees. Sending them to training

00:29:50.200 --> 00:29:54.789
opportunities is not always the best thing,
because they may pick up bad habits, so I

00:29:54.789 --> 00:29:59.200
think we have to go right back to the way
we train in order to actually embed this as

00:29:59.200 --> 00:30:03.120
we move forward and make sure it is business
as usual for the next generation coming through,

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:07.680
actually.
Muir Gray: Some patients are institutionalised;

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:11.440
all professionals are institutionalised. They
have mostly worked with the one hospital or

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:17.240
the one health centre for 25 years. We see
the STPs and the academic health science networks,

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:22.782
if we can get them at that strategic level,
get the map on the wall, so we need to get

00:30:22.782 --> 00:30:26.909
people out of their bunkers in the way we
have been doing, just to see what they are

00:30:26.909 --> 00:30:34.549
doing down the road; they have not a clue.
Joanne Shaw: I wanted to ask about the patient

00:30:34.549 --> 00:30:41.869
dimension. You said that your aspiration is
that nobody should have an intervention without

00:30:41.869 --> 00:30:46.349
having had a properly informed conversation,
to make a properly informed choice. I was

00:30:46.349 --> 00:30:51.780
interested in how this data is contributing
to that, because if I am thinking about a

00:30:51.780 --> 00:30:56.640
child potentially having their tonsils out,
and in one place, there is one chance in four

00:30:56.640 --> 00:31:00.110
they will be back in hospital again within
a month, and in another place, there are three

00:31:00.110 --> 00:31:06.330
chances in a hundred, that is a massively
different conversation. I suppose what we

00:31:06.330 --> 00:31:15.340
know is that another real driver for change,
as well as peer-to-peer and manager-to-practitioner,

00:31:15.340 --> 00:31:21.070
is patient-to-practitioner, and you only need
a few patients asking you about your complication

00:31:21.070 --> 00:31:26.220
rates to really begin to stimulate changes
in behaviour. I guess my question is, to what

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:30.331
extent is this data available to the public?
To what extent are we helping them use it?

00:31:30.331 --> 00:31:34.280
Muir Gray: Let me just comment: perhaps it
is the medium rather than the message. Bill

00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:38.360
Gates said the key question for any organisation
in the digital age is, what is the function

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:43.760
of the human being? What do want nurses and
doctors to do? It is not to give information.

00:31:43.770 --> 00:31:47.620
We know they are not very good at it, and
their consultants are so emotionally highly

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:53.720
charged, it is very difficult for people to
remember what happens. I think last month,

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:59.340
the usual figure is 35 million visits to NHS
Choices, and a shared decision-making programme

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:05.539
in there, so we need to work out new ways
of using digital before and after the consultation,

00:32:05.539 --> 00:32:10.809
and not rely on the face-to-face consultation
alone; it is just not working. We have now

00:32:10.809 --> 00:32:16.220
got the message, and we have the media, and
we have been doing a bit of a jump through

00:32:16.220 --> 00:32:21.270
general practice, through the information
systems, but I think that is what we see now,

00:32:21.270 --> 00:32:25.260
is, we need to move from ‘what is the matter’
medicine, as someone said, to ‘what matters

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:29.640
to you’ medicine. What actually is wrong
with you, that you think a knee replacement

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:35.200
would help? Usually it will, but there may
be other solutions. That is the plan, using

00:32:35.200 --> 00:32:41.940
digital resources to bring the patients, the
citizens, fully involved.

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:47.860
Tim Briggs: I think we should probably go
further than that. We are going to have this

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:51.420
dashboard showing data, and I want to get
it up and running; I want to make sure it

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:56.440
is robust, so we get it across 30 lines of
different specialties, which will be, as I

00:32:56.440 --> 00:33:02.610
say, 92% of what is going on in the provider
sector. I have been keeping the Patients Association,

00:33:02.610 --> 00:33:07.330
Katherine Murphy, involved in information
about this throughout. She has been incredibly

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:12.450
supportive, I have to say. Once we have got
the data robust, why can we not let CCGs and

00:33:12.450 --> 00:33:18.240
patients see it? That is going to be the question,
and I think that will drive the change even

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:23.350
further than we have driven the change now.
I think, eventually, in terms of transparency

00:33:23.350 --> 00:33:28.340
and the transparency agenda, that is what
will happen, and that is what I would aim

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:33.330
to do once we have things robust, in place,
and everybody is happy with their dataset.

00:33:33.330 --> 00:33:37.520
Joanne Shaw: If I might just jump in again,
I think the question is not, why can we not

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:43.000
let CCGs and the public, it is what possible
justification could there be to now allow

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:48.800
that to happen. Whereas in an ideal world,
we would clearly want every piece of data

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:54.290
that is put in the public domain to be completely
robust and reliable, in practice, I think

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:59.419
in a number of different spheres, one can
see that the knowledge that the data is going

00:33:59.419 --> 00:34:04.440
to become public is a massive stimulant to
people putting in the effort to make sure

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:10.020
it is right. I would just encourage you; I
think it is fantastic, and the sooner the better.

00:34:15.720 --> 00:34:18.760
John Burn: Muir, you mentioned warfarin briefly

00:34:18.760 --> 00:34:22.000
in passing, and I know you are very interested
in atrial fibrillation, which more than a

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:28.190
million people are at increased stroke risk
because of. In our city last year, more than

00:34:28.190 --> 00:34:31.820
half the people with AF who presented with
a stroke were not anticoagulated, so there

00:34:31.820 --> 00:34:37.992
is still a lot to be done in that domain.
In fact, because of publicity, more and more

00:34:37.992 --> 00:34:42.859
people are being put onto anticoagulants.
However, there is a big debate about, what

00:34:42.859 --> 00:34:48.200
anticoagulants? Warfarin is the standard,
but there is huge variation in who uses that

00:34:48.200 --> 00:34:53.990
versus the direct-acting oral anticoagulants.
That has an importance for us as a Board because,

00:34:53.990 --> 00:34:59.810
on a year-on-year growth of about £100 million
a year in expenditure, there is actually a

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:05.770
credible academic debate about whether in
fact warfarin used better by personalising

00:35:05.770 --> 00:35:13.690
the dose and by giving self-testing is actually
at least as good as DOACs. We have an argument

00:35:13.690 --> 00:35:18.010
not only about how do we get people anticoagulated,
but also which anticoagulant we use, because

00:35:18.010 --> 00:35:22.490
clearly, to make best use of resources, we
should be using the cheaper option. I have

00:35:22.490 --> 00:35:28.800
in front of me the open prescribing graph,
which shows a sevenfold variation across CCGs

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:38.480
in the ratio of warfarin to DOACs, rising
from 9% to 60%-odd. Is this an area that RightCare

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:42.380
might address, and what ideas do you have
on that?

00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:49.780
Muir Gray: This is moving from primary/secondary/tertiary
to people with cardiovascular disease, and

00:35:49.780 --> 00:35:54.270
within that, people with heart failure or
people with rhythm disorders, so it is a matrix,

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:59.020
not one or the other. As we move towards a
systems-based approach – and this is one

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:03.880
of the ones with Public Health England we
have set up a system – simply putting guidelines

00:36:03.880 --> 00:36:08.670
into a lot of bureaucracies will not solve
the problem. The bureaucracies are very good

00:36:08.670 --> 00:36:14.410
at linear tasks, like the fair and open employment
of people, but this is a non-linear problem.

00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:19.300
About a third of England is now signed up
to work to a common spec, and that would mean

00:36:19.310 --> 00:36:25.390
within the resources available, what are we
going to do? A key issue here is the centre,

00:36:25.390 --> 00:36:31.240
us, not telling people how to do it, but this
is classic military: strategy and operational

00:36:31.250 --> 00:36:35.500
is different, so we need to decide, what is
the right thing to do, and I will wait outside

00:36:35.500 --> 00:36:40.821
the meeting and get more information from
you. Then we meet the system spec. People

00:36:40.821 --> 00:36:45.970
locally; it is GPs in Bradford, it is a haematologist
in Essex with nurses, and it is a cardiologist

00:36:45.970 --> 00:36:52.530
in North London. That is a local operational
thing, but we NHS England, NHS Improvement,

00:36:52.530 --> 00:36:56.250
have to say what it is we want to achieve.
That would be a perfect example with no more

00:36:56.250 --> 00:37:01.740
resources, or if we make a decision to increase
resources, shall we shift money from another

00:37:01.740 --> 00:37:05.970
part of the cardiovascular budget, because
there are resources available in there of

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:11.170
lower value. I always use the words vascular
dementia as well as stroke, because I think

00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:16.720
10% of vascular dementia can be prevented
by the good management of atrial fibrillation.

00:37:16.740 --> 00:37:23.420
Michelle Mitchell: What would your message
to the public be about why this is a good

00:37:23.420 --> 00:37:31.780
thing rather than something to fear?
Muir Gray: I am a bit more in town halls than

00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:35.180
hospitals: maybe 200 times in the last year.
I like to meet with clinicians, patients and

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:41.200
the public. I show them, there is a wonderful
graph; the most important picture in medicine

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:45.620
is Donabedian’s graph. As you put money
in, the benefits go up, and then they flatten

00:37:45.620 --> 00:37:51.420
off. Harm goes up in a straight line. The
more healthcare we do, the more harm we do.

00:37:51.430 --> 00:37:57.900
Quality means benefit goes up and harm goes
down. In the middle, there is J-shaped curve;

00:37:57.900 --> 00:38:03.950
a point of optimality, Donabedian called it.
I ask the public, ‘Where do you know healthcare

00:38:03.950 --> 00:38:10.350
that has gone beyond the point of optimality?’
From the audiences, the same things come out:

00:38:10.350 --> 00:38:17.760
polypharmacy for older people, over-intervention
in the last year of life. This is not to do

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:23.540
with the money; sometimes chemotherapy, eighth-
or ninth-round chemotherapy, sometimes elective

00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:30.580
surgery, wonderful though it is. Too much
imaging. The public understand this; we need

00:38:30.580 --> 00:38:36.960
to get our story clearer, for both individuals
and the populations to make clear that all

00:38:36.960 --> 00:38:42.410
healthcare does harm as well as good, and
we need to treat you all as adults and we

00:38:42.410 --> 00:38:45.780
need to tell you that doctors usually use
less healthcare than non-doctors. We know

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:53.760
it goes wrong in the best of hands. Cataract
operations, one in 1,000 goes badly wrong.

00:38:54.740 --> 00:38:59.100
If you are housebound, then you run that risk,
but if it just picked up in the optometrist’s

00:38:59.109 --> 00:39:03.000
and you are put on a waiting list, you need
to know that is the option you are facing.

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:10.420
I find the public actually, if I am in Barnet
Town Hall or somewhere, are aware of this,

00:39:10.420 --> 00:39:13.820
but we have not articulated it clearly enough.
Tim Briggs: What I would say to that is, that

00:39:13.830 --> 00:39:19.480
when patients come to see me, they just want
to get better, and they want the best possible

00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:27.500
care. We try and achieve that at the organisation
in which I live. Now, what I want to see out

00:39:27.500 --> 00:39:34.980
of this is, if someone goes into hospital,
either for a prostate operation or a cholecystectomy,

00:39:34.981 --> 00:39:40.040
or a hip or knee replacement, they are going
to get the same outcome which is the highest

00:39:40.040 --> 00:39:44.990
quality, in the organisation across the country
which they go to. They are going to get the

00:39:44.990 --> 00:39:49.680
lowest complication rate, and as a taxpayer,
they are going to get it at the best value

00:39:49.680 --> 00:39:56.230
and in timely time. That is all patients want.
Actually, patients are very grateful to the

00:39:56.230 --> 00:40:00.820
NHS, I have to say, and I think it is a great
organisation; I have to say, I am totally

00:40:00.820 --> 00:40:05.380
supportive. Why? Because I am a consultant
who works in it. I am going to be a patient

00:40:05.380 --> 00:40:09.480
one day, so I want to make sure it actually
works really well, but I am also a taxpayer,

00:40:09.480 --> 00:40:13.790
so I want to make sure it is efficient, and
I have two kids doing medicine; I want to

00:40:13.790 --> 00:40:18.210
make sure they have a Health Service they
can be proud of, as I have been. Patients,

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:24.750
actually, are not difficult; patients just
want great outcomes, the right advice, low

00:40:24.750 --> 00:40:30.280
complication, and they can get on out there
with their lives. There is too much variation

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:36.369
out there to actually sit on our laurels and
say, ‘It cannot be improved.’ We can make

00:40:36.369 --> 00:40:41.400
a massive difference within the budget that
we have. If you said to me, ‘If you split

00:40:41.400 --> 00:40:44.890
social care out, do we need more money,’
not from where I am sitting; it has gone to

00:40:44.890 --> 00:40:50.640
265 hospitals. There is huge waste out there
that we can actually improve, and actually,

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:55.910
if we get it right – this will deliver,
this programme, I promise you – with the

00:40:55.910 --> 00:41:01.610
clinical leaders we have, we just apply for
19 more clinical leads. We have had over 50

00:41:01.610 --> 00:41:08.820
applicants, most of them absolutely top drawer.
By working together across NHSI and NHSE,

00:41:08.820 --> 00:41:12.330
I work with a great team; I work with a guy
called Professor Tim Evans, who is the National

00:41:12.330 --> 00:41:17.320
Director of Productivity. Fantastic guy who
gets it. I work with Jeremy Marlow, who is

00:41:17.320 --> 00:41:22.640
a civil servant, who is fantastic. We get
it, and the whole team wants to deliver it.

00:41:22.640 --> 00:41:28.360
If you said to me, ‘How can you help us
in NHSE,’ two things. One is, to help with

00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:32.400
levers, to drive the change. I know tariffs
are fixed for two years, but when we come

00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:36.349
to you and say, ‘We need some commissioning
guidance out there, we need it now and this

00:41:36.349 --> 00:41:39.060
is what we are going to do,’ I would like
to see an urgent response so we can drive

00:41:39.060 --> 00:41:45.400
that. We do not want to duplicate and triplicate
effort. We are driving the GIRFT programme,

00:41:45.400 --> 00:41:49.010
we are going to have all the data that can
inform your specialised commissioning, CCGs

00:41:49.010 --> 00:41:53.630
already phoning us up for data, working with
Muir, but what we do not want to do is have

00:41:53.630 --> 00:41:59.099
duplicates and triplicates, and sometimes,
I think across the two systems, that is happening,

00:41:59.099 --> 00:42:03.430
and we need to squeeze that out. There are
teams here that are trying to do what we are

00:42:03.430 --> 00:42:07.310
doing, but we are way ahead of the game. Use
that resource somewhere else, and just come

00:42:07.310 --> 00:42:11.970
to us for the data. Between us, I think we
can make a massive difference.

00:42:32.220 --> 00:42:36.540
Moira Gibb: Really, really encouraging, positive
story, and obviously necessary work. All the

00:42:36.540 --> 00:42:43.010
examples you have given us are from physical
medicine. Given that we aim for parity of

00:42:43.010 --> 00:42:47.310
esteem in relation to mental health, does
your remit cover mental health, and could

00:42:47.310 --> 00:42:54.690
you give us some examples in that field?
Muir Gray: When we are on the road – this

00:42:54.690 --> 00:42:59.359
is to do with starting with allocative value
– I usually show the figure, so respiratory,

00:42:59.359 --> 00:43:05.380
£5 billion; cancer, £7 billion; skin, £2
billion; how much do you think we spend on

00:43:05.380 --> 00:43:10.380
mental health? Then I ask the room to vote,
and this will include a room with finance

00:43:10.380 --> 00:43:16.290
staff and mental health professionals. There
is silence in the room, and then I offer them,

00:43:16.290 --> 00:43:19.910
£1 billion to £4 billion, £4 billion to
£8 billion, or over £8 billion. Usually,

00:43:19.910 --> 00:43:23.970
80% of the room say it is £1 billion to £4
billion, or under £1 billion. The answer,

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:29.710
of course, is about £12 billion a year. Now,
it may be that it should be more, but what

00:43:29.710 --> 00:43:35.261
are we getting for £12 billion? It is actually
quite hard to see that, if you look at the

00:43:35.261 --> 00:43:39.880
annual reports of trusts, because they have
big lumps of things like adult or community,

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:46.960
£190 million or £64 million, and we do not
have a team working in that way at the moment.

00:43:46.960 --> 00:43:50.220
That is the first issue, I think, is to be
much clearer. I know it is more complex, mental

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:55.560
health is more complex, but we have to apply
the same principles of how much is allocated,

00:43:55.560 --> 00:44:02.250
and there is 1.9-fold variation in what CCGs
allocate to mental health. It varies from

00:44:02.250 --> 00:44:09.099
about 1.6 to 1.9 over the years, and there
may be a 0.1 here or there, data handling,

00:44:09.099 --> 00:44:15.510
but huge, unwarranted variation, so I would
see this as a key issue, and I think there

00:44:15.510 --> 00:44:21.150
are certainly high-value activities that could
be done more of, but we have to engage with

00:44:21.150 --> 00:44:26.470
patients, the public and the professionals
to look at unwarranted variation, and then

00:44:26.470 --> 00:44:30.240
to look at the outcomes and the quality of
what is provided.

00:44:30.240 --> 00:44:34.920
I will give you a very simple example. I ask
people, ‘How much is spent on depression

00:44:34.920 --> 00:44:40.940
in this population?’ Silence. ‘How many
people are there with depression in Barsetshire?’

00:44:40.940 --> 00:44:48.420
Silence. Then asking the specialists, ‘How
much do the GPs spend?’ Silence. We need

00:44:48.430 --> 00:44:51.790
to get the map on the wall, and that is where
I think the STPs are at the right sort of

00:44:51.790 --> 00:44:58.590
level. We are taking this approach, and it
is the most complex area, but the same questions

00:44:58.590 --> 00:45:01.210
apply.
Tim Briggs: If I can just comment on that

00:45:01.210 --> 00:45:07.640
too, actually. In my discussions with David
Williams, the Financial General of DH, we

00:45:07.640 --> 00:45:12.410
have agreed to appoint a national GIRFT lead
in mental health. I have had conversations

00:45:12.410 --> 00:45:15.970
with the President of the Royal College of
Psychiatrists, we have already got a business

00:45:15.970 --> 00:45:20.690
plan together, and we will be appointing our
30th specialty, which will be mental health.

00:45:20.690 --> 00:45:24.960
At the moment, I cannot give you some examples
like I have with the others, because we have

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:28.670
not done the data-mining, but we know with
things like rehabilitation, there are massive

00:45:28.670 --> 00:45:33.450
gains to be had in that. Watch this space,
but there will be a GIRFT national lead in

00:45:33.450 --> 00:45:39.840
mental health.
Malcolm Grant: I think the Board has had an

00:45:39.849 --> 00:45:44.730
extremely valuable insight into the work that
is happening both in GIRFT and in RightCare.

00:45:44.730 --> 00:45:49.160
It is quite an eye-opener, and I think you
are absolutely right to present the findings

00:45:49.160 --> 00:45:55.350
as starkly as you do, with the extraordinary
variation. I think your message to us, which

00:45:55.350 --> 00:46:00.500
is about allocative efficiency, as well – perhaps
culturally more difficult for us to absorb

00:46:00.500 --> 00:46:05.099
– is something which we will certainly take
on board. Can I on behalf of the Board not

00:46:05.099 --> 00:46:08.560
just thank you for coming, but actually for
the energy and the application that has gone

00:46:08.560 --> 00:46:13.550
into the work so far, and to the potential
implications. Of course, the potential implications

00:46:13.550 --> 00:46:17.800
are not just limited to what we do in the
NHS in this country; these are global. Of

00:46:17.800 --> 00:46:23.520
course, the Atlas comes from Jack Wennberg,
originally. I think this is an extraordinarily

00:46:23.520 --> 00:46:28.460
valuable insight at a time when there is not
a sustainable healthcare system in the modern

00:46:28.470 --> 00:46:32.590
developed world.
Muir Gray: We will be pleased to speak to

00:46:32.590 --> 00:46:39.650
any individual members of the Board. You know
where we live.

00:46:39.650 --> 00:46:42.839
Malcolm Grant: We know where you live, but
also, I think the point that both you and

00:46:42.839 --> 00:46:47.930
Tim were making around the STP process as
being a way of trying to take on particularly

00:46:47.930 --> 00:46:52.300
the allocative efficiency issues of this is
absolutely critical. We will not only know

00:46:52.300 --> 00:46:57.030
where you live, but we will be knocking on
your door and drawing on your expertise, so

00:46:57.030 --> 00:47:01.930
thank you again very much. Again, thanks to
Matthew Cripps and the rest of the members

00:47:01.930 --> 00:47:05.440
of the team who have been doing RightCare
on our behalf.

